The control on consent calls for tools or mechanisms that let individuals consent to the processing of their personally identifiable information prior to its collection, so they can make informed decisions [PT-4]. Whether consent is mandatory depends on context. It may be required by applicable laws, executive orders, directives, regulations, policies, standards, or guidelines [PT-4]. Where it isn't, organizations should consider whether individuals can reasonably be expected to understand and accept the privacy risks, and whether other controls would mitigate the risk better, alone or alongside consent [PT-4].

If you do use consent, the control says to consider:
- the type of consent, such as opt-in or opt-out;
- how to authenticate and identity proof individuals;
- how to obtain consent electronically;
- a way to revoke consent;
- plain language without technical jargon [PT-4].
